The correct approach: count multiples of 12 (41), 20 (25), 30 (16), sum 82. Numbers divisible by all three are multiples of 60, of which there are 8; each was counted 3 times in the sum, but should be counted once. So subtract 2 per each: 82 − 16 = 66.

The proposed solution first correctly gets 82 − 2(8) = 66, then bizarrely reverts to subtracting 3 per triple (82 − 24 = 58), which undercounts. The verification on n ≤ 60 is also flawed: per 60-block the correct count is 10 − 2(1) = 8 (numbers 12, 20, 24, 30, 36, 40, 48, 60 — indeed 8, matching 66's structure, not 58). Scaling 58 from that block is inconsistent arithmetic anyway.

